---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";

const implementation = [
  'bisection <- function(f, a, b, tol = 1e-3, m = 100) {',
  '    iter <- 0',
  '    f.a <- f(a)',
  '    f.b <- f(b)',
  '',
  '    while (abs(b - a) > tol) {',
  '        iter <- iter + 1',
  '        if (iter > m) {',
  '            warning("iterations maximum exceeded")',
  '            break',
  '        }',
  '        xmid <- (a + b) / 2',
  '        ymid <- f(xmid)',
  '        if (f.a * ymid > 0) {',
  '            a <- xmid',
  '            f.a <- ymid',
  '        } else {',
  '            b <- xmid',
  '            f.b <- ymid',
  '        }',
  '    }',
  '',
  '    root <- (a + b) / 2',
  '    return(root)',
  '}',
].join("\n");

const arbitraryExample = `f <- function(x) {
  exp(-x) - x
}`;
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Bisection Method — CMNA"
  description="Learn and experiment with the bisection method as implemented in CMNA."
>
  <header class="page-head">
    <div class="shell">
      <p class="eyebrow">Root finding · CMNA Laboratory</p>
      <h1 class="page-title">The bisection method</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Bracket a real root, halve the interval, keep the half that still contains
        the sign change, and repeat. It is difficult to make glamorous, which is
        precisely why it is so useful.
      </p>
    </div>
  </header>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell two-column">
      <article class="content">
        <h2>The idea</h2>
        <p>
          Suppose a continuous function changes sign between <code>a</code> and
          <code>b</code>. The intermediate value theorem tells us that at least one
          real root lies between them. Bisection evaluates the midpoint and discards
          the half-interval that cannot contain the sign change.
        </p>
        <p>
          Each successful iteration cuts the width of the bracket in half. After
          <em>n</em> steps, the surviving interval is only
          <code>(b − a) / 2^n</code> wide. That makes the method slower than some
          alternatives, but unusually transparent: the computation carries its own
          error bound around with it.
        </p>

        <div class="callout">
          <strong>What bisection buys you:</strong> if the starting interval really
          brackets a sign change and the function is continuous, the method does not
          need a clever initial guess or a derivative. It just keeps making the
          uncertainty smaller.
        </div>
      </article>

      <div class="code-panel">
        <pre><code>{implementation}</code></pre>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section lab-section">
    <div class="shell" data-bisection-lab>
      <div class="lab-heading">
        <div>
          <p class="section-kicker">Experiment</p>
          <h2 class="section-title">Watch the bracket collapse.</h2>
          <p class="lede">
            Choose a function and tolerance, then scrub through the iterations.
            The Murrey endpoints mark the current bracket; the gold point is the
            midpoint tested at that step.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="lab-expression">
          <span>f(x)</span>
          <code data-expression></code>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="lab-controls">
        <label>
          Function
          <select data-preset>
            <option value="cubic">x³ − x − 2</option>
            <option value="fixed">cos(x) − x</option>
            <option value="sqrt2">x² − 2</option>
            <option value="book">CMNA cubic</option>
          </select>
        </label>
        <label>
          Tolerance
          <select data-tolerance>
            <option value="0.1">10⁻¹</option>
            <option value="0.01">10⁻²</option>
            <option value="0.001" selected>10⁻³</option>
            <option value="0.000001">10⁻⁶</option>
          </select>
        </label>
        <button class="button lab-button" type="button" data-run>Run</button>
      </div>

      <div class="lab-summary" data-summary></div>
      <p class="lab-status" data-status></p>

      <div class="lab-plot-frame">
        <svg
          class="lab-plot"
          role="img"
          aria-label="Graph of the selected function with the current bisection interval"
          data-plot
        ></svg>
      </div>

      <div class="lab-stepper">
        <button class="button secondary-on-light" type="button" data-play>Play convergence</button>
        <label>
          <span data-step-label>Iteration</span>
          <input type="range" min="0" max="1" value="0" data-step />
        </label>
      </div>

      <div class="lab-table-wrap">
        <table class="lab-table">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>n</th>
              <th>a</th>
              <th>b</th>
              <th>midpoint</th>
              <th>f(midpoint)</th>
              <th>width</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody data-rows></tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>


  <section class="section r-guided-section">
    <div class="shell" data-bisection-r-lab>
      <div class="lab-heading">
        <div>
          <p class="section-kicker">Bring your own function</p>
          <h2 class="section-title">Now remove the training wheels.</h2>
          <p class="lede">
            Define <code>f</code> as any valid R function, choose an interval, and let
            the browser run the same bisection logic against your problem. The point
            here is not merely to get a root: CMNA will unpack why the interval works,
            what the first sign test does, how the uncertainty shrinks, and exactly
            why the algorithm stops.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="lab-expression r-badge-panel">
          <span>Runtime</span>
          <code data-r-status>Preparing R…</code>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="r-guided-grid">
        <section class="r-guided-editor">
          <div class="r-editor-label">
            <span>R</span>
            <small>Define a function named <code>f</code></small>
          </div>
          <textarea
            class="r-editor r-function-editor"
            data-r-function
            spellcheck="false"
            autocomplete="off"
            aria-label="Arbitrary R function for bisection"
          >{arbitraryExample}</textarea>

          <div class="r-guided-controls">
            <label>
              a
              <input type="number" value="0" step="any" data-r-a />
            </label>
            <label>
              b
              <input type="number" value="1" step="any" data-r-b />
            </label>
            <label>
              tolerance
              <input type="number" value="0.000001" min="0" step="any" data-r-tol />
            </label>
          </div>

          <div class="r-run-row">
            <button class="button r-run" type="button" data-r-bisect disabled>Run this R function</button>
            <button class="r-reset" type="button" data-r-reset>Reset R session</button>
          </div>
        </section>

        <aside class="r-guided-primer">
          <h3>What has to be true first?</h3>
          <p>
            Bisection is not magic. The method needs a continuous function and an
            interval whose endpoint values have opposite signs. If your function does
            not satisfy that condition, the laboratory will stop there and explain
            why rather than pretending the algorithm succeeded.
          </p>
          <div class="mini-proof">
            <span>sign f(a)</span>
            <strong>≠</strong>
            <span>sign f(b)</span>
            <strong>⇒</strong>
            <span>root in [a,b]</span>
          </div>
        </aside>
      </div>

      <div class="lab-summary" data-r-summary></div>
      <div class="teaching-sequence" data-r-explanation></div>

      <div class="lab-plot-frame">
        <svg
          class="lab-plot r-bisection-plot"
          role="img"
          aria-label="Graph of the arbitrary R function with the current bisection interval"
          data-r-plot
        ></svg>
      </div>

      <div class="lab-stepper">
        <button class="button secondary-on-light" type="button" data-r-play disabled>Play convergence</button>
        <label>
          <span data-r-step-label>Iteration</span>
          <input type="range" min="0" max="1" value="0" data-r-step disabled />
        </label>
      </div>
      <p class="lab-status" data-r-step-detail></p>

      <div class="lab-table-wrap">
        <table class="lab-table">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>n</th>
              <th>a</th>
              <th>b</th>
              <th>midpoint</th>
              <th>f(midpoint)</th>
              <th>width</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody data-r-rows></tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p class="workbench-source-note">
        The function and trace above are evaluated by real R through webR. The
        teaching trace instruments the current CMNA bisection update rule; the
        canonical <code>bisection()</code> function itself remains unchanged.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell two-column">
      <article class="content">
        <h2>Where bisection sits among root finders</h2>
        <p>
          Newton's method can converge much faster when a useful derivative and
          starting value are available. The secant method estimates that derivative
          from nearby function values. Bisection trades that speed for a remarkably
          simple guarantee: preserve the sign-changing bracket and the search region
          keeps shrinking.
        </p>
        <p>
          <a href="/methods/root-finding/">Compare bisection, Newton, and secant on the same functions →</a>
        </p>
      </article>

      <aside class="callout">
        <strong>Source of truth</strong>
        <p>
          The code shown above follows the current <code>cmna</code> package source.
          The laboratory is a browser-side visualization of the same update rule, not
          a replacement implementation for production work.
        </p>
        <a href="https://github.com/k3jph/cmna-pkg/blob/main/R/bisection.R">View bisection.R on GitHub →</a><br />
        <a href="/workbench/">Or take bisection into the full R Workbench →</a>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </section>

  <script is:inline type="module" src="/js/bisection-lab.js"></script>
  <script is:inline type="module" src="/js/bisection-r-lab.js"></script>
</BaseLayout>
